X:1
T:Llannerch-y-medd
R:cân
S:https://amgueddfa.cymru/casgliadau/caneuongwerin/?id=19 (collected by D. Roy Saer)
N:Sung by T. Morris Owen (born 1887), a Ministry of Defence officer, of 15 Goat Street, St David's, Pembrokeshire; recorded 12.9.63 (St Fagans tape 69). A native of Anglesey, he first heard it between the ages of four and six, when he was being brought up at his grandmother's home in Llanfachreth, about seven miles from Holyhead.
N:A triban that praises the village of Llannerch-y-medd in northern Anglesey as the burial place of King Pabo, one of the Celtic saints, and of a queen. Nothing is known of the basis for the tradition: the inscription "HIC JACET PABO POST PRIID ..." is in fact three miles away, at the church of Llanbabo. The tune is a version of "Distyll y Don". With slight changes it can be used for any stanza in triban metre.
